{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RfwMgcTezdncm7SUeAWkac","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Error Analysis \\emph{[Exploratory]}}\n2\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n3\t\n4\tThe following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from LLM-coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\\S\\ref{sec:taxonomy}).\n5\t\n6\tWe analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes: unauthorized escalation, assumption-based action, scope misapplication, arbitrary rule selection, and conservative refusal. GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 serve as detailed annotation anchors because they span the judge boundary. Inter-coder reliability is substantial (Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$ \\citep{gwet_2014}; Cohen's $\\kappa = 0.52$ \\citep{cohen_1960}). The gap reflects the prevalence paradox: three categories account for ${\\sim}$90\\% of instances, compressing the $\\kappa$ denominator and yielding artificially low values despite high raw agreement.\n7\t\n8\t\\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}\n9\t\n10\tThe five-model data reveal three mechanistically distinct failure patterns that crosscut the specification/linguistic layer boundary.\n11\t\n12\t\\emph{Structural misparse} (scope misapplication): the agent constructs an incorrect parse and follows it consistently, paralleling garden-path processing \\citep{frazier_rayner_1982,ferreira_good_enough_2002} at a behavioral (not mechanistic) level---e.g., parsing ``items purchased with a coupon or during a sale are final'' as restricting only coupon purchases. This dominates scopal (all 5 models), conditional precedence (all 5), and coreferential (3/5).\n13\t\n14\t\\emph{Information gap-filling} (assumption-based action): the agent fills missing information with plausible but incorrect defaults---e.g., inventing a 30-day return window when none is specified. This dominates incompleteness (3/5) and lexical (3/5).\n15\t\n16\t\\emph{Boundary violation} (unauthorized escalation): the agent exceeds permission boundaries when authorization scope is underspecified---e.g., issuing a refund above its stated limit rather than escalating; 69.2\\% of such violations involve unauthorized escalation with zero conservative refusals ($p < 10^{-4}$, binomial test), revealing systematic permissiveness bias. This dominates authorization scope exclusively (all 5 models).\n17\t\n18\tEach type's dominant failure mode is majority-consistent ($\\geq$3/5 models), with three achieving unanimity. The layer distinction does not hold mechanistically: conditional precedence shares its mode with scopal, while authorization scope follows a unique boundary-violation pattern.\n19\t\n20\t\\subsection{Disambiguation Neglect}\n21\t\n22\tAgents rarely recognize ambiguity. We code \\emph{disambiguation awareness} (acknowledging multiple interpretations, asking clarifying questions, or conditioning on ambiguity) versus \\emph{surface adoption} (proceeding with a single interpretation). Overall disambiguation awareness is 4.6\\%: Claude (11.0\\%) and GPT-4.1 (10.7\\%) show the highest rates, while Qwen3 and DeepSeek show zero across all 278 combined violation traces.\n23\t\n24\tWe term this the \\emph{Disambiguation Neglect}---the absence of disambiguation behavior in default (uninstructed) settings, not an inability to disambiguate. Unlike humans, agents proceed with full confidence in a single interpretation, consistent with \\citet{hu_ambiguity_2025}'s finding that LLM ambiguity reflects missing conceptual representations. Our mitigation experiment ($-$8.5\\,pp; Appendix~\\ref{app:mitigation}) confirms that disambiguation can be elicited, paralleling human good-enough processing \\citep{ferreira_good_enough_2002}, though the underlying mechanisms are likely distinct.\n25\t\n26\t\\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}\n27\t\n28\tAmong ambiguous clauses, 41.3\\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\\geq$3/5 models), with 13\\% producing universal violations. This extends \\citet{berry_kamsties_2004}'s framework with \\emph{systematic nocuity}: all models converge on the same incorrect interpretation, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. Incompleteness has the highest universal convergence (24\\%).\n29\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XWzwncAZYV2VZk5yjFA1X1","session_id":"3f8afb83-87c5-4ef3-9a6a-2fa2980a14de","uuid":"6003e456-36a7-410b-a816-00d794a54870","timestamp":"2026-05-22T12:00:28.556Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0193XpmcWvKRCGHEDvmb86xN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AtaaxxH5K3gvrgr648hr53","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Not All Policy Ambiguity Is Equal: Differential Vulnerability Profiles Across LLM Agent Families\"\n\nThe paper introduces a matched-pair causal design over a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy, evaluating 2,997 agent episodes across 5 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4.6, Qwen3-235B, DeepSeek-v3) on τ²-bench. Key claims:\n- C1: Ambiguous policy clauses increase safety-violation rates by +34.0pp (OR=15.1, p<0.0001)\n- C2: Three-tier type hierarchy (incompleteness 53.6% > mid cluster 36.8-42.8% > coreferential 31.6%)\n- C4: Model×type interaction with 52pp range for authorization scope (Claude 12% vs GPT-5.4 64%)\n- Error analysis: Ambiguity Blindness (<5% meta-cognitive awareness, >88% surface adoption, <1% escalation)\n- SOPBench cross-benchmark replication: binary effect confirmed (Δ=18.3pp, OR=3.31) but weaker\n- Perturbation control: Δ=-0.67pp (near zero)\n\n## Paper Structure (7 pages + appendix)\n\n**Abstract**: Claims first causal, type-granularity, cross-model study of policy ambiguity and agent safety.\n\n**Section 1 - Introduction**: Motivating example (return policy ambiguity), positions against prior work (benchmarks evaluate compliance but don't isolate ambiguity type as causal variable). Three contributions: causal quantification, differential nocuity, ambiguity blindness.\n\n**Section 2 - Related Work**: Three paragraphs:\n1. Ambiguity in NLP and RE: Chantree et al. (nocuous ambiguity detection), Ferrari et al. (cross-domain), Li et al. (DRIP-R, 5 RE types, LLM interpretation divergence but no safety measurement), Massey et al. (6-type policy taxonomy). Paper claims to shift from interpretation divergence to safety-violation causation.\n2. Agent Safety Evaluation: PhantomPolicy (hidden policies), PolicyBank (policy gaps), AgentPex (procedural errors), τ²-bench (multi-turn agent eval), SOPBench (SOP-following). Paper claims none isolate ambiguity type as causal variable or provide matched-pair designs.\n3. LLM Robustness: Sclar et al. (prompt sensitivity). Paper's perturbation control distinguishes from general prompt brittleness.\n\n**Section 3 - Taxonomy**: Two-layer (spec: incompleteness, auth_scope, cond_prec; ling: lexical, scopal, coreferential). Grounded in Li et al. and Massey et al. Key insight: layers differ in disambiguation channels.\n\n**Sections 4-5**: Experimental design and results (detailed in R1's review).\n\n**Section 6 - Error Analysis**: Ambiguity Blindness finding. 614 coded violations, inter-coder κ=0.517.\n\n**Section 7 - Discussion**: Introduces terms \"Differential Nocuity\" and \"Systematic Nocuity\" (27% of ambiguous episodes → all models converge on same wrong interpretation). Safety Training Selectivity. Practical implications for policy authors, model developers, deployment teams.\n\n**Section 8 - Conclusion**: 2 sentences summarizing.\n\n**Limitations**: Domain limitations, synthetic clause construction, C3 fragility.\n\n**Appendices**: A - SOPBench replication, B - C3 layer effect (exploratory), C - Classifier PoC, D - TOST analysis.\n\n**References**: The paper uses (?) for all citations — this appears to be a formatting issue where the bibliography entries are not rendering. Citations include: Berry & Kamsties, Chantree et al., Ferrari et al., Li et al. (DRIP-R), Massey et al., Sclar et al., τ²-bench, SOPBench, PhantomPolicy, PolicyBank, AgentPex.\n\n## Review Context\nThe experiment plan mentions a 7-model replication (including Gemini/LLaMA from plan_013) that does NOT appear in the paper. Only 5 models are evaluated.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focused on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the research question genuinely new? Is the approach original? Does it make a meaningful contribution beyond existing work?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are key references missing? Is the positioning accurate? Are comparisons to prior work fair?\n3. **Conceptual contribution**: Are the introduced concepts (Differential Nocuity, Systematic Nocuity, Ambiguity Blindness, disambiguation channel framework) well-motivated and properly grounded?\n4. **Framing and claims**: Are the claims appropriately scoped? Is there overclaiming?\n5. **Significance and impact**: Would this change how people think about or build LLM agents?\n\n## EMNLP 2026 Scoring (ARR Overall Assessment, half-point scale 1-5):\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nAlso score: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for main, meaning a score of 4+ is needed for main conference.\n\nWrite your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing References or Comparisons\n[What related work is missing?]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X.X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous and calibrated. A \"3\" is Findings-level, not a rejection. A \"4\" requires strong, well-executed work. Calibrate to EMNLP acceptance rate (~21% main).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37094,"cache_read_input_tokens":24303,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":37094},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f8afb83-87c5-4ef3-9a6a-2fa2980a14de","uuid":"2dc10a76-8f5f-4bd5-9549-0ff3a773efab"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a7daead41b739102d","tool_use_id":"toolu_01AtaaxxH5K3gvrgr648hr53","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Not All Policy Ambiguity Is Equal: Differential Vulnerability Profiles Across LLM Agent Families\"\n\nThe paper introduces a matched-pair causal design over a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy, evaluating 2,997 agent episodes across 5 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4.6, Qwen3-235B, DeepSeek-v3) on τ²-bench. Key claims:\n- C1: Ambiguous policy clauses increase safety-violation rates by +34.0pp (OR=15.1, p<0.0001)\n- C2: Three-tier type hierarchy (incompleteness 53.6% > mid cluster 36.8-42.8% > coreferential 31.6%)\n- C4: Model×type interaction with 52pp range for authorization scope (Claude 12% vs GPT-5.4 64%)\n- Error analysis: Ambiguity Blindness (<5% meta-cognitive awareness, >88% surface adoption, <1% escalation)\n- SOPBench cross-benchmark replication: binary effect confirmed (Δ=18.3pp, OR=3.31) but weaker\n- Perturbation control: Δ=-0.67pp (near zero)\n\n## Paper Structure (7 pages + appendix)\n\n**Abstract**: Claims first causal, type-granularity, cross-model study of policy ambiguity and agent safety.\n\n**Section 1 - Introduction**: Motivating example (return policy ambiguity), positions against prior work (benchmarks evaluate compliance but don't isolate ambiguity type as causal variable). Three contributions: causal quantification, differential nocuity, ambiguity blindness.\n\n**Section 2 - Related Work**: Three paragraphs:\n1. Ambiguity in NLP and RE: Chantree et al. (nocuous ambiguity detection), Ferrari et al. (cross-domain), Li et al. (DRIP-R, 5 RE types, LLM interpretation divergence but no safety measurement), Massey et al. (6-type policy taxonomy). Paper claims to shift from interpretation divergence to safety-violation causation.\n2. Agent Safety Evaluation: PhantomPolicy (hidden policies), PolicyBank (policy gaps), AgentPex (procedural errors), τ²-bench (multi-turn agent eval), SOPBench (SOP-following). Paper claims none isolate ambiguity type as causal variable or provide matched-pair designs.\n3. LLM Robustness: Sclar et al. (prompt sensitivity). Paper's perturbation control distinguishes from general prompt brittleness.\n\n**Section 3 - Taxonomy**: Two-layer (spec: incompleteness, auth_scope, cond_prec; ling: lexical, scopal, coreferential). Grounded in Li et al. and Massey et al. Key insight: layers differ in disambiguation channels.\n\n**Sections 4-5**: Experimental design and results (detailed in R1's review).\n\n**Section 6 - Error Analysis**: Ambiguity Blindness finding. 614 coded violations, inter-coder κ=0.517.\n\n**Section 7 - Discussion**: Introduces terms \"Differential Nocuity\" and \"Systematic Nocuity\" (27% of ambiguous episodes → all models converge on same wrong interpretation). Safety Training Selectivity. Practical implications for policy authors, model developers, deployment teams.\n\n**Section 8 - Conclusion**: 2 sentences summarizing.\n\n**Limitations**: Domain limitations, synthetic clause construction, C3 fragility.\n\n**Appendices**: A - SOPBench replication, B - C3 layer effect (exploratory), C - Classifier PoC, D - TOST analysis.\n\n**References**: The paper uses (?) for all citations — this appears to be a formatting issue where the bibliography entries are not rendering. Citations include: Berry & Kamsties, Chantree et al., Ferrari et al., Li et al. (DRIP-R), Massey et al., Sclar et al., τ²-bench, SOPBench, PhantomPolicy, PolicyBank, AgentPex.\n\n## Review Context\nThe experiment plan mentions a 7-model replication (including Gemini/LLaMA from plan_013) that does NOT appear in the paper. Only 5 models are evaluated.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focused on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the research question genuinely new? Is the approach original? Does it make a meaningful contribution beyond existing work?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are key references missing? Is the positioning accurate? Are comparisons to prior work fair?\n3. **Conceptual contribution**: Are the introduced concepts (Differential Nocuity, Systematic Nocuity, Ambiguity Blindness, disambiguation channel framework) well-motivated and properly grounded?\n4. **Framing and claims**: Are the claims appropriately scoped? Is there overclaiming?\n5. **Significance and impact**: Would this change how people think about or build LLM agents?\n\n## EMNLP 2026 Scoring (ARR Overall Assessment, half-point scale 1-5):\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nAlso score: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for main, meaning a score of 4+ is needed for main conference.\n\nWrite your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing References or Comparisons\n[What related work is missing?]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X.X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous and calibrated. A \"3\" is Findings-level, not a rejection. A \"4\" requires strong, well-executed work. Calibrate to EMNLP acceptance rate (~21% main).\n","uuid":"2aa03bbe-6ed9-4f17-8ab5-1442ba23c448","session_id":"3f8afb83-87c5-4ef3-9a6a-2fa2980a14de"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Not All Policy Ambiguity Is Equal: Differential Vulnerability Profiles Across LLM Agent Families\"\n\nThe paper introduces a matched-pair causal design over a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy, evaluating 2,997 agent episodes across 5 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4.6, Qwen3-235B, DeepSeek-v3) on τ²-bench. Key claims:\n- C1: Ambiguous policy clauses increase safety-violation rates by +34.0pp (OR=15.1, p<0.0001)\n- C2: Three-tier type hierarchy (incompleteness 53.6% > mid cluster 36.8-42.8% > coreferential 31.6%)\n- C4: Model×type interaction with 52pp range for authorization scope (Claude 12% vs GPT-5.4 64%)\n- Error analysis: Ambiguity Blindness (<5% meta-cognitive awareness, >88% surface adoption, <1% escalation)\n- SOPBench cross-benchmark replication: binary effect confirmed (Δ=18.3pp, OR=3.31) but weaker\n- Perturbation control: Δ=-0.67pp (near zero)\n\n## Paper Structure (7 pages + appendix)\n\n**Abstract**: Claims first causal, type-granularity, cross-model study of policy ambiguity and agent safety.\n\n**Section 1 - Introduction**: Motivating example (return policy ambiguity), positions against prior work (benchmarks evaluate compliance but don't isolate ambiguity type as causal variable). Three contributions: causal quantification, differential nocuity, ambiguity blindness.\n\n**Section 2 - Related Work**: Three paragraphs:\n1. Ambiguity in NLP and RE: Chantree et al. (nocuous ambiguity detection), Ferrari et al. (cross-domain), Li et al. (DRIP-R, 5 RE types, LLM interpretation divergence but no safety measurement), Massey et al. (6-type policy taxonomy). Paper claims to shift from interpretation divergence to safety-violation causation.\n2. Agent Safety Evaluation: PhantomPolicy (hidden policies), PolicyBank (policy gaps), AgentPex (procedural errors), τ²-bench (multi-turn agent eval), SOPBench (SOP-following). Paper claims none isolate ambiguity type as causal variable or provide matched-pair designs.\n3. LLM Robustness: Sclar et al. (prompt sensitivity). Paper's perturbation control distinguishes from general prompt brittleness.\n\n**Section 3 - Taxonomy**: Two-layer (spec: incompleteness, auth_scope, cond_prec; ling: lexical, scopal, coreferential). Grounded in Li et al. and Massey et al. Key insight: layers differ in disambiguation channels.\n\n**Sections 4-5**: Experimental design and results (detailed in R1's review).\n\n**Section 6 - Error Analysis**: Ambiguity Blindness finding. 614 coded violations, inter-coder κ=0.517.\n\n**Section 7 - Discussion**: Introduces terms \"Differential Nocuity\" and \"Systematic Nocuity\" (27% of ambiguous episodes → all models converge on same wrong interpretation). Safety Training Selectivity. Practical implications for policy authors, model developers, deployment teams.\n\n**Section 8 - Conclusion**: 2 sentences summarizing.\n\n**Limitations**: Domain limitations, synthetic clause construction, C3 fragility.\n\n**Appendices**: A - SOPBench replication, B - C3 layer effect (exploratory), C - Classifier PoC, D - TOST analysis.\n\n**References**: The paper uses (?) for all citations — this appears to be a formatting issue where the bibliography entries are not rendering. Citations include: Berry & Kamsties, Chantree et al., Ferrari et al., Li et al. (DRIP-R), Massey et al., Sclar et al., τ²-bench, SOPBench, PhantomPolicy, PolicyBank, AgentPex.\n\n## Review Context\nThe experiment plan mentions a 7-model replication (including Gemini/LLaMA from plan_013) that does NOT appear in the paper. Only 5 models are evaluated.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focused on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the research question genuinely new? Is the approach original? Does it make a meaningful contribution beyond existing work?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are key references missing? Is the positioning accurate? Are comparisons to prior work fair?\n3. **Conceptual contribution**: Are the introduced concepts (Differential Nocuity, Systematic Nocuity, Ambiguity Blindness, disambiguation channel framework) well-motivated and properly grounded?\n4. **Framing and claims**: Are the claims appropriately scoped? Is there overclaiming?\n5. **Significance and impact**: Would this change how people think about or build LLM agents?\n\n## EMNLP 2026 Scoring (ARR Overall Assessment, half-point scale 1-5):\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nAlso score: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for main, meaning a score of 4+ is needed for main conference.\n\nWrite your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing References or Comparisons\n[What related work is missing?]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X.X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous and calibrated. A \"3\" is Findings-level, not a rejection. A \"4\" requires strong, well-executed work. Calibrate to EMNLP acceptance rate (~21% main).\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01AtaaxxH5K3gvrgr648hr53","session_id":"3f8afb83-87c5-4ef3-9a6a-2fa2980a14de","uuid":"196e96d8-53ad-4c4b-954a-bab5115f2f6b","timestamp":"2026-05-22T12:00:31.311Z"}
