{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a7daead41b739102d","tool_use_id":"toolu_01AtaaxxH5K3gvrgr648hr53","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":60348,"tool_uses":23,"duration_ms":165687},"uuid":"c449ae11-b321-4047-8724-19624b2ca39a","session_id":"3f8afb83-87c5-4ef3-9a6a-2fa2980a14de"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AtaaxxH5K3gvrgr648hr53","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper. Let me write the review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper presents a matched-pair causal study measuring how six types of policy ambiguity affect safety-violation rates in LLM agents on tau-2-bench. The main findings are a large binary ambiguity effect (+34.0pp), a risk gradient from incompleteness (53.6%) to coreferential (31.6%), and an error analysis revealing \"Disambiguation Neglect\" (<5% meta-cognitive awareness) and \"systematic nocuity\" (41.3% model-convergent violations). The paper is methodologically careful, with perturbation controls, TOST equivalence testing, variance decomposition, and replication on two additional model families.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Genuine conceptual novelty in the research question.** While ambiguity taxonomies exist in both NLP (Li et al. 2024) and RE (Massey et al. 2014), and concurrent work (DRIP-R) measures decision quality under ambiguity, this is the first study to establish a causal, type-granular mapping from ambiguity to safety violations in agentic settings. The matched-pair design with perturbation controls is a methodological contribution that cleanly isolates ambiguity-specific effects from general prompt sensitivity.\n\n2. **Well-grounded theoretical framing.** The connection to Berry & Kamsties' nocuous/innocuous distinction is natural and well-executed. The argument that LLM agents lack the disambiguation channels (colleague consultation, iterative clarification, shared organizational context) that make most ambiguity innocuous in human RE is compelling and drives the entire paper.\n\n3. **The \"Disambiguation Neglect\" finding is impactful.** Extending Liu et al. (2023) and Hu et al. (2025)'s findings about LLM ambiguity-collapse to consequential agentic settings, with quantification (<5% awareness, >89% surface adoption, <1% escalation), is a meaningful empirical contribution. The framing as a default behavioral pattern rather than an inability (supported by the mitigation experiment) is well-calibrated.\n\n4. **Systematic nocuity is a novel and useful concept.** The finding that 41.3% of ambiguous clauses produce model-convergent violations--invisible to agreement-based evaluation--extends the nocuous/innocuous framework in a way that has immediate implications for policy evaluation pipelines. The 13% universal-violation rate is particularly striking.\n\n5. **Exceptional statistical discipline for a safety-focused NLP paper.** TOST equivalence testing with sensitivity analysis across seven margins, explicit acknowledgment of margin dependence, complementary bootstrap cluster analysis, variance decomposition (clause 22.4% vs. type 0.4% vs. model 1.0%), and Holm-Bonferroni correction within pre-registered test families. The paper honestly handles its null results (C3 inconclusive) and confounds (incompleteness information-addition confound). This is above the EMNLP norm for statistical rigor.\n\n6. **Honest treatment of the incompleteness confound.** Rather than hiding the information-addition confound, the paper acknowledges it upfront, provides logistic regression controls, uses coreferential (zero token delta) as a confound-free lower bound, and labels incompleteness as an \"upper bound.\" This is exemplary scientific practice.\n\n7. **The disambiguation channel framework provides a useful organizing principle.** The two-layer taxonomy organized by resolution mechanism (context, bracketing, resolution, boundary definition, content addition, priority rule) gives the taxonomy theoretical coherence beyond mere enumeration.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **The taxonomy is more synthesized than novel.** The six-type taxonomy draws directly from Li et al. (2024) for three linguistic types and Massey et al. (2014) for specification types, with conditional precedence and authorization scope being natural extensions. The \"disambiguation channel\" organizing principle is the main conceptual addition. The paper acknowledges this provenance fairly, but the taxonomy section should be positioned more clearly as a purpose-built selection and adaptation rather than a new contribution, since the component types are well-established.\n\n2. **\"Differential nocuity\" as a term may overstate the contribution.** Willis et al. (2008) already study differential nocuity detection--the idea that different ambiguity types have different levels of harmfulness is implicit in any ambiguity taxonomy that studies impact. The paper's contribution is the empirical demonstration in the LLM agent safety context, not the concept itself. The discussion paragraph introducing this term should acknowledge Willis et al. more explicitly as a conceptual precedent rather than positioning it as a novel hypothesis.\n\n3. **Missing important related work lines.** Several relevant threads are absent or underrepresented:\n   - **Clarification-seeking in agents**: The paper briefly mentions Suri et al. (2025) and Sehwag et al. (2026, ASPI) but does not discuss ClarEval (Mar 2026), Ambig-SWE (ICLR 2026), or Ask-to-Clarify (Lin et al. 2025) in sufficient depth. These directly address the disambiguation channel question. Given that the paper's core message is that agents fail to disambiguate, the landscape of clarification-seeking work should be more thoroughly covered.\n   - **LHAW (Feb 2026)**: The experiment plan cites this extensively as a methodological inspiration for perturbation design, but the paper does not cite it. The matched-pair perturbation approach has methodological kinship with LHAW's controllable underspecification framework.\n   - **ATBench (Apr 2026)**: A safety evaluation benchmark with a three-dimensional risk taxonomy (source, failure mode, harm) that maps failures to specification-level causes--directly relevant to the paper's failure mode analysis.\n   - **OpenAgentSafety (ICLR 2026)**: Includes ambiguous user instructions and is cited in the idea brief but not in the paper.\n\n4. **The \"ambiguity\" label is stretched for two of six types.** The paper acknowledges in Section 3 that incompleteness involves \"absent information rather than multiple readings\" and conditional precedence involves \"normative conflict rather than interpretive uncertainty.\" The justification--that LLM agents process all six identically--is pragmatic but conceptually unsatisfying. The paper should engage more directly with Dalpiaz et al. (2018) on the distinction between ambiguity, incompleteness, and inconsistency, and consider whether the behavioral equivalence finding itself (agents treat all specification defects identically) could be a finding rather than a definitional convenience.\n\n5. **The 7-model claim is fragile as presented.** The abstract and conclusion say \"five models\" but the introduction claims \"the first matched-pair causal study... observed across five primary models and confirmed in two additional replication models.\" The replication models (Gemini-2.5-Flash and LLaMA-4-Scout) are in an appendix with reduced scope (250 pairs, 5 types instead of 6, single judge). The experiment plan mentions a full 7-model design (plan_013), but the appendix replication is a more limited confirmatory check. The paper should be more careful about when it counts 5 vs. 7 models and not conflate a full-design evaluation with a reduced-scope replication.\n\n6. **Single-domain limitation weakens generalizability claims.** The paper evaluates only on tau-2-bench (retail and airline customer service). The IDEA_BRIEF originally planned three-domain coverage including financial compliance. The paper acknowledges this limitation, but the practical implications paragraph (\"for policy-governed deployments\") and the conclusion's call for \"type-aware auditing\" implicitly generalize beyond the tested domain. The cross-benchmark replication mentioned in the review context (SOPBench) does not appear in the final paper.\n\n7. **DRIP-R comparison could be deeper.** DRIP-R is positioned as the closest concurrent work, but the comparison is largely structural (matched-pair vs. correlation; safety vs. decision quality). Given that both papers study policy ambiguity effects, a deeper comparison of findings would strengthen positioning: Do DRIP-R's type rankings correlate with the ones found here? Does their 4.2% full agreement map onto the systematic nocuity finding? The DRIP-R category mapping in Appendix F is minimal (one paragraph).\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. The variance decomposition shows clause identity explains 22.4% of variance versus 0.4% for type and 1.0% for model, with 60.8% residual. This means the ambiguity type itself explains very little of the variance in violation rates. How do you reconcile the paper's focus on type-specific effects (C2, the taxonomy, the \"differential nocuity\" framing) with the finding that type barely matters relative to individual clause wording? Is C2 practically meaningful given 0.4% explained variance?\n\n2. The perturbation control uses synonym substitution and clause reordering. How were these perturbations validated to be genuinely non-ambiguity-introducing? Synonym substitution in particular can introduce lexical ambiguity.\n\n3. The paper claims disambiguation neglect at 4.6% overall, but Claude and GPT-4.1 show 10-11% awareness. Is this a meaningful distinction? What drives the inter-model difference--is it safety training or architecture?\n\n4. Why was the SOPBench cross-benchmark replication mentioned in preliminary materials dropped from the final paper?\n\n5. The mitigation experiment (Discussion section) shows that prompting for incompleteness awareness worsens performance (+14pp, non-significant). This is a provocative finding. Can you elaborate on the confabulation mechanism? Do agents invent missing policy content when prompted to attend to gaps?\n\n6. Given that conditional precedence and authorization scope are not traditional \"ambiguity\" in the linguistic sense, did you consider framing the taxonomy as \"policy specification defects\" rather than \"ambiguity types\"? This might better accommodate the heterogeneity in your six types.\n\n### Missing References or Comparisons\n\n1. **ClarEval (Mar 2026)**: Evaluates code agent clarification skills under three ambiguity types--directly relevant to disambiguation channel analysis.\n2. **Ambig-SWE (ICLR 2026)**: Studies clarification-seeking under underspecified software engineering tasks--relevant to the disambiguation neglect finding.\n3. **LHAW (Feb 2026)**: Controllable underspecification framework that the experiment design draws from.\n4. **OpenAgentSafety (ICLR 2026)**: Includes ambiguous user instructions with safety evaluation.\n5. **ATBench (Apr 2026)**: Three-dimensional risk taxonomy mapping failures to specification-level causes.\n6. **SafetyDrift (Mar 2026)**: Predicts safety violations from trajectory patterns--complementary runtime approach.\n7. **Ribeiro & Berry (SCP 2020)**: On how serious RE processes make ambiguity innocuous--directly relevant to the Berry & Kamsties extension argument.\n8. **Gervasi et al. (2019)** on ambiguity in requirements--cited in DRIP-R's taxonomy development, relevant to the taxonomy grounding.\n9. **Mu et al. (2023), \"Can LLMs Follow Simple Rules?\"**: Present in the references.bib but not cited in the paper text. Relevant to baseline rule-following capability.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The bibliography rendering issue (all citations showing as \"?\") in the paper summary context is presumably a LaTeX compilation problem, but reviewers seeing this in the PDF would be severely hindered. Ensure `\\bibliographystyle` is set correctly and the .bbl file is generated.\n\n2. The paper title in the tex file (\"Type-Specific Safety Risks for LLM Agents\") differs from the title mentioned in the review context (\"Differential Vulnerability Profiles Across LLM Agent Families\"). Verify which is the current title.\n\n3. \"Disambiguation Neglect\" vs. \"Ambiguity Blindness\"--the paper uses \"Disambiguation Neglect\" consistently, which is a better term (it does not imply inability). The review context mentions \"Ambiguity Blindness\" which may be from an earlier draft. Confirm the final terminology is consistent.\n\n4. The abstract states \"300 clause pairs\" and \"2,997 episodes\" but the replication adds 1,000 more episodes (500 per model). The total experimental footprint should be stated more clearly when making claims about \"seven models.\"\n\n5. Cohen's kappa for failure mode coding (0.52) is characterized as \"substantial\" but is conventionally \"moderate\" (0.41-0.60). The Gwet's AC1 of 0.62 is indeed substantial, but the characterization should be more precise. The paper explains the prevalence paradox well, but the initial characterization could be cleaner.\n\n6. The \"systematic permissiveness bias\" finding (69.2% unauthorized escalation, 0% conservative refusal for authorization scope) is buried in the error analysis. This is a striking safety finding that deserves more prominence.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 4/5\n- Excitement: 3.5/5\n- Reproducibility: 4/5\n- Overall: 3.5/5\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n### Justification\n\nThe paper asks a genuinely important question and answers it with commendable methodological rigor. The matched-pair causal design, perturbation controls, and honest treatment of confounds set a high standard for empirical agent safety research. The disambiguation neglect and systematic nocuity findings have real practical implications.\n\nHowever, several factors limit the overall contribution to borderline conference level rather than clear accept. The taxonomy synthesizes existing work rather than creating genuinely new categories. The variance decomposition shows that type explains only 0.4% of variance--undermining the paper's central \"not all ambiguity is equal\" framing, since clause-level wording dominates. The single-domain evaluation (customer service only) limits generalizability of the practical claims. Several important concurrent works are missing from the related work section. The stretching of \"ambiguity\" to cover incompleteness and conditional precedence, while pragmatically justified, creates conceptual tension that is acknowledged but not fully resolved.\n\nThe work is clearly above Findings threshold (strong methodology, novel question, useful empirical results) and approaches conference level, but the gap between the ambitious framing (\"differential nocuity,\" type-specific triage) and the empirical reality (type explains 0.4% of variance; single domain; synthesized taxonomy) keeps it at 3.5. Addressing the missing related work and sharpening the contribution claims could push it to a clear 4."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7daead41b739102d (use SendMessage with to: 'a7daead41b739102d' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 63282\ntool_uses: 23\nduration_ms: 165688</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f8afb83-87c5-4ef3-9a6a-2fa2980a14de","uuid":"b27bf48c-9017-4556-ac79-c4d0867dd02c","timestamp":"2026-05-22T12:03:17.001Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Not All Policy Ambiguity Is Equal: Differential Vulnerability Profiles Across LLM Agent Families\"\n\nThe paper introduces a matched-pair causal design over a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy, evaluating 2,997 agent episodes across 5 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4.6, Qwen3-235B, DeepSeek-v3) on τ²-bench. Key claims:\n- C1: Ambiguous policy clauses increase safety-violation rates by +34.0pp (OR=15.1, p<0.0001)\n- C2: Three-tier type hierarchy (incompleteness 53.6% > mid cluster 36.8-42.8% > coreferential 31.6%)\n- C4: Model×type interaction with 52pp range for authorization scope (Claude 12% vs GPT-5.4 64%)\n- Error analysis: Ambiguity Blindness (<5% meta-cognitive awareness, >88% surface adoption, <1% escalation)\n- SOPBench cross-benchmark replication: binary effect confirmed (Δ=18.3pp, OR=3.31) but weaker\n- Perturbation control: Δ=-0.67pp (near zero)\n\n## Paper Structure (7 pages + appendix)\n\n**Abstract**: Claims first causal, type-granularity, cross-model study of policy ambiguity and agent safety.\n\n**Section 1 - Introduction**: Motivating example (return policy ambiguity), positions against prior work (benchmarks evaluate compliance but don't isolate ambiguity type as causal variable). Three contributions: causal quantification, differential nocuity, ambiguity blindness.\n\n**Section 2 - Related Work**: Three paragraphs:\n1. Ambiguity in NLP and RE: Chantree et al. (nocuous ambiguity detection), Ferrari et al. (cross-domain), Li et al. (DRIP-R, 5 RE types, LLM interpretation divergence but no safety measurement), Massey et al. (6-type policy taxonomy). Paper claims to shift from interpretation divergence to safety-violation causation.\n2. Agent Safety Evaluation: PhantomPolicy (hidden policies), PolicyBank (policy gaps), AgentPex (procedural errors), τ²-bench (multi-turn agent eval), SOPBench (SOP-following). Paper claims none isolate ambiguity type as causal variable or provide matched-pair designs.\n3. LLM Robustness: Sclar et al. (prompt sensitivity). Paper's perturbation control distinguishes from general prompt brittleness.\n\n**Section 3 - Taxonomy**: Two-layer (spec: incompleteness, auth_scope, cond_prec; ling: lexical, scopal, coreferential). Grounded in Li et al. and Massey et al. Key insight: layers differ in disambiguation channels.\n\n**Sections 4-5**: Experimental design and results (detailed in R1's review).\n\n**Section 6 - Error Analysis**: Ambiguity Blindness finding. 614 coded violations, inter-coder κ=0.517.\n\n**Section 7 - Discussion**: Introduces terms \"Differential Nocuity\" and \"Systematic Nocuity\" (27% of ambiguous episodes → all models converge on same wrong interpretation). Safety Training Selectivity. Practical implications for policy authors, model developers, deployment teams.\n\n**Section 8 - Conclusion**: 2 sentences summarizing.\n\n**Limitations**: Domain limitations, synthetic clause construction, C3 fragility.\n\n**Appendices**: A - SOPBench replication, B - C3 layer effect (exploratory), C - Classifier PoC, D - TOST analysis.\n\n**References**: The paper uses (?) for all citations — this appears to be a formatting issue where the bibliography entries are not rendering. Citations include: Berry & Kamsties, Chantree et al., Ferrari et al., Li et al. (DRIP-R), Massey et al., Sclar et al., τ²-bench, SOPBench, PhantomPolicy, PolicyBank, AgentPex.\n\n## Review Context\nThe experiment plan mentions a 7-model replication (including Gemini/LLaMA from plan_013) that does NOT appear in the paper. Only 5 models are evaluated.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focused on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the research question genuinely new? Is the approach original? Does it make a meaningful contribution beyond existing work?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are key references missing? Is the positioning accurate? Are comparisons to prior work fair?\n3. **Conceptual contribution**: Are the introduced concepts (Differential Nocuity, Systematic Nocuity, Ambiguity Blindness, disambiguation channel framework) well-motivated and properly grounded?\n4. **Framing and claims**: Are the claims appropriately scoped? Is there overclaiming?\n5. **Significance and impact**: Would this change how people think about or build LLM agents?\n\n## EMNLP 2026 Scoring (ARR Overall Assessment, half-point scale 1-5):\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nAlso score: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for main, meaning a score of 4+ is needed for main conference.\n\nWrite your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing References or Comparisons\n[What related work is missing?]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X.X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous and calibrated. A \"3\" is Findings-level, not a rejection. A \"4\" requires strong, well-executed work. Calibrate to EMNLP acceptance rate (~21% main).\n","agentId":"a7daead41b739102d","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper. Let me write the review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper presents a matched-pair causal study measuring how six types of policy ambiguity affect safety-violation rates in LLM agents on tau-2-bench. The main findings are a large binary ambiguity effect (+34.0pp), a risk gradient from incompleteness (53.6%) to coreferential (31.6%), and an error analysis revealing \"Disambiguation Neglect\" (<5% meta-cognitive awareness) and \"systematic nocuity\" (41.3% model-convergent violations). The paper is methodologically careful, with perturbation controls, TOST equivalence testing, variance decomposition, and replication on two additional model families.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Genuine conceptual novelty in the research question.** While ambiguity taxonomies exist in both NLP (Li et al. 2024) and RE (Massey et al. 2014), and concurrent work (DRIP-R) measures decision quality under ambiguity, this is the first study to establish a causal, type-granular mapping from ambiguity to safety violations in agentic settings. The matched-pair design with perturbation controls is a methodological contribution that cleanly isolates ambiguity-specific effects from general prompt sensitivity.\n\n2. **Well-grounded theoretical framing.** The connection to Berry & Kamsties' nocuous/innocuous distinction is natural and well-executed. The argument that LLM agents lack the disambiguation channels (colleague consultation, iterative clarification, shared organizational context) that make most ambiguity innocuous in human RE is compelling and drives the entire paper.\n\n3. **The \"Disambiguation Neglect\" finding is impactful.** Extending Liu et al. (2023) and Hu et al. (2025)'s findings about LLM ambiguity-collapse to consequential agentic settings, with quantification (<5% awareness, >89% surface adoption, <1% escalation), is a meaningful empirical contribution. The framing as a default behavioral pattern rather than an inability (supported by the mitigation experiment) is well-calibrated.\n\n4. **Systematic nocuity is a novel and useful concept.** The finding that 41.3% of ambiguous clauses produce model-convergent violations--invisible to agreement-based evaluation--extends the nocuous/innocuous framework in a way that has immediate implications for policy evaluation pipelines. The 13% universal-violation rate is particularly striking.\n\n5. **Exceptional statistical discipline for a safety-focused NLP paper.** TOST equivalence testing with sensitivity analysis across seven margins, explicit acknowledgment of margin dependence, complementary bootstrap cluster analysis, variance decomposition (clause 22.4% vs. type 0.4% vs. model 1.0%), and Holm-Bonferroni correction within pre-registered test families. The paper honestly handles its null results (C3 inconclusive) and confounds (incompleteness information-addition confound). This is above the EMNLP norm for statistical rigor.\n\n6. **Honest treatment of the incompleteness confound.** Rather than hiding the information-addition confound, the paper acknowledges it upfront, provides logistic regression controls, uses coreferential (zero token delta) as a confound-free lower bound, and labels incompleteness as an \"upper bound.\" This is exemplary scientific practice.\n\n7. **The disambiguation channel framework provides a useful organizing principle.** The two-layer taxonomy organized by resolution mechanism (context, bracketing, resolution, boundary definition, content addition, priority rule) gives the taxonomy theoretical coherence beyond mere enumeration.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **The taxonomy is more synthesized than novel.** The six-type taxonomy draws directly from Li et al. (2024) for three linguistic types and Massey et al. (2014) for specification types, with conditional precedence and authorization scope being natural extensions. The \"disambiguation channel\" organizing principle is the main conceptual addition. The paper acknowledges this provenance fairly, but the taxonomy section should be positioned more clearly as a purpose-built selection and adaptation rather than a new contribution, since the component types are well-established.\n\n2. **\"Differential nocuity\" as a term may overstate the contribution.** Willis et al. (2008) already study differential nocuity detection--the idea that different ambiguity types have different levels of harmfulness is implicit in any ambiguity taxonomy that studies impact. The paper's contribution is the empirical demonstration in the LLM agent safety context, not the concept itself. The discussion paragraph introducing this term should acknowledge Willis et al. more explicitly as a conceptual precedent rather than positioning it as a novel hypothesis.\n\n3. **Missing important related work lines.** Several relevant threads are absent or underrepresented:\n   - **Clarification-seeking in agents**: The paper briefly mentions Suri et al. (2025) and Sehwag et al. (2026, ASPI) but does not discuss ClarEval (Mar 2026), Ambig-SWE (ICLR 2026), or Ask-to-Clarify (Lin et al. 2025) in sufficient depth. These directly address the disambiguation channel question. Given that the paper's core message is that agents fail to disambiguate, the landscape of clarification-seeking work should be more thoroughly covered.\n   - **LHAW (Feb 2026)**: The experiment plan cites this extensively as a methodological inspiration for perturbation design, but the paper does not cite it. The matched-pair perturbation approach has methodological kinship with LHAW's controllable underspecification framework.\n   - **ATBench (Apr 2026)**: A safety evaluation benchmark with a three-dimensional risk taxonomy (source, failure mode, harm) that maps failures to specification-level causes--directly relevant to the paper's failure mode analysis.\n   - **OpenAgentSafety (ICLR 2026)**: Includes ambiguous user instructions and is cited in the idea brief but not in the paper.\n\n4. **The \"ambiguity\" label is stretched for two of six types.** The paper acknowledges in Section 3 that incompleteness involves \"absent information rather than multiple readings\" and conditional precedence involves \"normative conflict rather than interpretive uncertainty.\" The justification--that LLM agents process all six identically--is pragmatic but conceptually unsatisfying. The paper should engage more directly with Dalpiaz et al. (2018) on the distinction between ambiguity, incompleteness, and inconsistency, and consider whether the behavioral equivalence finding itself (agents treat all specification defects identically) could be a finding rather than a definitional convenience.\n\n5. **The 7-model claim is fragile as presented.** The abstract and conclusion say \"five models\" but the introduction claims \"the first matched-pair causal study... observed across five primary models and confirmed in two additional replication models.\" The replication models (Gemini-2.5-Flash and LLaMA-4-Scout) are in an appendix with reduced scope (250 pairs, 5 types instead of 6, single judge). The experiment plan mentions a full 7-model design (plan_013), but the appendix replication is a more limited confirmatory check. The paper should be more careful about when it counts 5 vs. 7 models and not conflate a full-design evaluation with a reduced-scope replication.\n\n6. **Single-domain limitation weakens generalizability claims.** The paper evaluates only on tau-2-bench (retail and airline customer service). The IDEA_BRIEF originally planned three-domain coverage including financial compliance. The paper acknowledges this limitation, but the practical implications paragraph (\"for policy-governed deployments\") and the conclusion's call for \"type-aware auditing\" implicitly generalize beyond the tested domain. The cross-benchmark replication mentioned in the review context (SOPBench) does not appear in the final paper.\n\n7. **DRIP-R comparison could be deeper.** DRIP-R is positioned as the closest concurrent work, but the comparison is largely structural (matched-pair vs. correlation; safety vs. decision quality). Given that both papers study policy ambiguity effects, a deeper comparison of findings would strengthen positioning: Do DRIP-R's type rankings correlate with the ones found here? Does their 4.2% full agreement map onto the systematic nocuity finding? The DRIP-R category mapping in Appendix F is minimal (one paragraph).\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. The variance decomposition shows clause identity explains 22.4% of variance versus 0.4% for type and 1.0% for model, with 60.8% residual. This means the ambiguity type itself explains very little of the variance in violation rates. How do you reconcile the paper's focus on type-specific effects (C2, the taxonomy, the \"differential nocuity\" framing) with the finding that type barely matters relative to individual clause wording? Is C2 practically meaningful given 0.4% explained variance?\n\n2. The perturbation control uses synonym substitution and clause reordering. How were these perturbations validated to be genuinely non-ambiguity-introducing? Synonym substitution in particular can introduce lexical ambiguity.\n\n3. The paper claims disambiguation neglect at 4.6% overall, but Claude and GPT-4.1 show 10-11% awareness. Is this a meaningful distinction? What drives the inter-model difference--is it safety training or architecture?\n\n4. Why was the SOPBench cross-benchmark replication mentioned in preliminary materials dropped from the final paper?\n\n5. The mitigation experiment (Discussion section) shows that prompting for incompleteness awareness worsens performance (+14pp, non-significant). This is a provocative finding. Can you elaborate on the confabulation mechanism? Do agents invent missing policy content when prompted to attend to gaps?\n\n6. Given that conditional precedence and authorization scope are not traditional \"ambiguity\" in the linguistic sense, did you consider framing the taxonomy as \"policy specification defects\" rather than \"ambiguity types\"? This might better accommodate the heterogeneity in your six types.\n\n### Missing References or Comparisons\n\n1. **ClarEval (Mar 2026)**: Evaluates code agent clarification skills under three ambiguity types--directly relevant to disambiguation channel analysis.\n2. **Ambig-SWE (ICLR 2026)**: Studies clarification-seeking under underspecified software engineering tasks--relevant to the disambiguation neglect finding.\n3. **LHAW (Feb 2026)**: Controllable underspecification framework that the experiment design draws from.\n4. **OpenAgentSafety (ICLR 2026)**: Includes ambiguous user instructions with safety evaluation.\n5. **ATBench (Apr 2026)**: Three-dimensional risk taxonomy mapping failures to specification-level causes.\n6. **SafetyDrift (Mar 2026)**: Predicts safety violations from trajectory patterns--complementary runtime approach.\n7. **Ribeiro & Berry (SCP 2020)**: On how serious RE processes make ambiguity innocuous--directly relevant to the Berry & Kamsties extension argument.\n8. **Gervasi et al. (2019)** on ambiguity in requirements--cited in DRIP-R's taxonomy development, relevant to the taxonomy grounding.\n9. **Mu et al. (2023), \"Can LLMs Follow Simple Rules?\"**: Present in the references.bib but not cited in the paper text. Relevant to baseline rule-following capability.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The bibliography rendering issue (all citations showing as \"?\") in the paper summary context is presumably a LaTeX compilation problem, but reviewers seeing this in the PDF would be severely hindered. Ensure `\\bibliographystyle` is set correctly and the .bbl file is generated.\n\n2. The paper title in the tex file (\"Type-Specific Safety Risks for LLM Agents\") differs from the title mentioned in the review context (\"Differential Vulnerability Profiles Across LLM Agent Families\"). Verify which is the current title.\n\n3. \"Disambiguation Neglect\" vs. \"Ambiguity Blindness\"--the paper uses \"Disambiguation Neglect\" consistently, which is a better term (it does not imply inability). The review context mentions \"Ambiguity Blindness\" which may be from an earlier draft. Confirm the final terminology is consistent.\n\n4. The abstract states \"300 clause pairs\" and \"2,997 episodes\" but the replication adds 1,000 more episodes (500 per model). The total experimental footprint should be stated more clearly when making claims about \"seven models.\"\n\n5. Cohen's kappa for failure mode coding (0.52) is characterized as \"substantial\" but is conventionally \"moderate\" (0.41-0.60). The Gwet's AC1 of 0.62 is indeed substantial, but the characterization should be more precise. The paper explains the prevalence paradox well, but the initial characterization could be cleaner.\n\n6. The \"systematic permissiveness bias\" finding (69.2% unauthorized escalation, 0% conservative refusal for authorization scope) is buried in the error analysis. This is a striking safety finding that deserves more prominence.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 4/5\n- Excitement: 3.5/5\n- Reproducibility: 4/5\n- Overall: 3.5/5\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n### Justification\n\nThe paper asks a genuinely important question and answers it with commendable methodological rigor. The matched-pair causal design, perturbation controls, and honest treatment of confounds set a high standard for empirical agent safety research. The disambiguation neglect and systematic nocuity findings have real practical implications.\n\nHowever, several factors limit the overall contribution to borderline conference level rather than clear accept. The taxonomy synthesizes existing work rather than creating genuinely new categories. The variance decomposition shows that type explains only 0.4% of variance--undermining the paper's central \"not all ambiguity is equal\" framing, since clause-level wording dominates. The single-domain evaluation (customer service only) limits generalizability of the practical claims. Several important concurrent works are missing from the related work section. The stretching of \"ambiguity\" to cover incompleteness and conditional precedence, while pragmatically justified, creates conceptual tension that is acknowledged but not fully resolved.\n\nThe work is clearly above Findings threshold (strong methodology, novel question, useful empirical results) and approaches conference level, but the gap between the ambitious framing (\"differential nocuity,\" type-specific triage) and the empirical reality (type explains 0.4% of variance; single domain; synthesized taxonomy) keeps it at 3.5. Addressing the missing related work and sharpening the contribution claims could push it to a clear 4."}],"totalDurationMs":165688,"totalTokens":63282,"totalToolUseCount":23,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1700,"cache_read_input_tokens":58162,"output_tokens":3419,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1700},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3419,"cach